{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"RESULT: FINDINGS\n\nI read the brief, the reference-led-design and react-bits-pro skills, the six named renders, and `site/v2/one/login.tsx`. One file note: `deck-login.png` on disk currently shows the orbit-of-logos composition (identical to p13), not the fan of three story cards the brief describes; I answer question 2 from his words and the accepted Deck feed, and flag this for the host.\n\n**1. Why Center Flow failed, in his words**\n\n- The effect: \"absolutely hate the effect this center flow produces. Despise it.\" Pulses, glow and orbiting lines are the same failure as the rejected wire feed: \"the flowchart feeding into the story, that thing. That's so stupid.\"\n- The frame: a glowing tech-demo object sits next to a product form. His settled rule: when he names a real object, that object goes in the frame; nothing from a catalog stands in for it. Center Flow is a catalog effect (`center-flow` in the React Bits list) carrying the content instead of being it.\n- The objects: a diagram of sources is not the product. What made pages alive for him: \"if it weren't for you adding the Vercel, Hugging Face, and all those logos on the left, it would have never looked so lively.\" Life is real logos on real stories, not lines between nodes. The skill's section 2a: an element that only shows how the system works inside fails.\n\n**2. The right side, two options ranked**\n\n**Option 1 (build this): the Deck stack, on its own ground.** Three real story cards from `deck/data.ts` (the Next.js 15 story with its image leading, one more with an image, one without), stacked exactly as accepted-deck.png: front card fully readable with its real source marks, two plates peeking behind offset, lifted with `--card-shadow` and `--top-light`, sitting right of the 420px form. Nothing moves. It reads as \"this is what you get\" because it is literally the feed's object (p14-feed) standing at the door. It must not become a fan half-hidden behind the form competing with it, a dimmed backdrop, or a React Bits `Stack` block dropped in unharmonized.\n\n**Option 2: one large story, sources on the card.** A single lifted story card, image and facts readable, with its five real source marks (GitHub, Product Hunt, web, RSS, X) as a static source row on the card, as the feeds already do it. Static. This honors his 21:15 words (\"1 story in the middle... from github product hunt web rss and X that is it\") with the sources as the story's own objects, no wires. Risk: one card alone may read thin beside the feeds' density.\n\nIs Deck's fan the answer as it stands? No. The instinct is right (\"I like the login for the Deck more\"), but cards fanned behind the form read as decoration, and the form must stay the lead; give the stack its own space with a readable front card.\n\n**3. The one to build**\n\nOption 1. The sentence to hold: the right side is the feed's own deck of real stories, static and readable, so he sees what he gets, never a diagram of how it works."}]},"session_id":"c45b5518-a766-440e-9a99-d1e0e1c465be"}
